# Shellius posture collector — managed by Shellius bootstrap, DO NOT EDIT.
#
# Separate unit from shellius-heartbeat.service and the JIT reaper
# (shellius-jit-reap.service) by hard requirement — a crashing or OOMing
# collector must never be able to break SSH authentication on a prod box.
# This unit is its own failure domain: it has no relationship to sshd's
# AuthorizedPrincipalsCommand, and a failure here only ever costs a
# missed posture snapshot (reported as a stale server in the UI), never a
# login. See docs/posture/posture-spec.md §1 decision 4 and §9.1.
#
# Runs as the unprivileged 'shellius-posture' system account, never root,
# never in the 'docker' group. Root-only reads (ss across all users' pids,
# ufw/firewalld state, systemctl show) go through the narrow sudoers
# drop-in at /etc/sudoers.d/shellius-posture — see that file for the exact
# command list. No Docker socket access at all: see
# shellius-posture-collect.sh's header for what that trades away and why.
[Unit]
Description=Shellius host posture collector
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=shellius-posture
Group=shellius-posture
ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/shellius-posture-report
# A collector that OOMs or spins must be a reported degradation, never a
# resource fight with anything else on the box (docs/posture/posture-spec.md
# §1 decision 4, §4).
MemoryMax=128M
CPUQuota=20%
# Hardening alongside the narrow sudoers grant.
#
# The privilege model of this unit IS that sudoers grant: the collector is
# unprivileged, and reaches root only through the fixed, argument-pinned
# commands in /etc/sudoers.d/shellius-posture. That model needs `sudo` to
# work — and sudo is a setuid binary. So this unit must NOT set anything that
# stops a setuid binary gaining privilege:
#
#   - NoNewPrivileges=true sets the kernel's no_new_privs flag, under which
#     setuid is ignored. It shipped here once, with a comment claiming it
#     "cannot gain new privileges beyond the sudoers grant" — it in fact
#     disabled the grant itself. Every `sudo -n` failed, the collector's
#     run_priv() hid the reason, and every host reported "Collector
#     degraded … sudo grant missing" with unknown owners and reachability.
#   - ProtectKernelModules= and RestrictSUIDSGID= install seccomp filters,
#     and older systemd releases implied NoNewPrivileges=yes for seccomp
#     options in a unit with a non-root User=. The fleet runs a mix of
#     distros, so they are out too.
#   - DynamicUser= implies it on every release. Not used; keep it that way.
#
# backend/src/routes/__tests__/postureUnit.test.js fails the build if any of
# these come back.
#
# What remains still matters: a private /tmp, /usr /boot /etc read-only, no
# writes to /home, a read-only /proc/sys.
#
# ProtectSystem=full (not strict) leaves /run writable, because the root
# commands sudo runs share this mount namespace — iptables-legacy takes
# /run/xtables.lock, and a read-only /run fails it outright.
#
# ProtectHome=read-only (not true): the collector reads pm2's saved app list
# from /root/.pm2 and /home/*/.pm2, and `true` makes those directories
# empty, so pm2 apps were never reported from a host. read-only restores the
# view; ordinary file permissions still decide what the unprivileged
# collector can actually open, so a 0700 /root or 0750 home stays private.
PrivateTmp=true
ProtectSystem=full
ProtectHome=read-only
ProtectKernelTunables=true
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=shellius-posture
TimeoutStartSec=60
